<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Moscow Peace Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1940</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Moscow Peace Treaty (1940) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Peace Treaty, signed on 12 March 1940, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Finland kept its independence but ceded about a tenth of its territory, including the Karelian Isthmus with the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Viipuri</a>, lands north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-ladoga">Lake Ladoga</a> and part of Salla, and leased the Hanko peninsula to the Soviets. Over 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> were evacuated from the ceded areas.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty.jpg" alt="Moscow Peace Treaty" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jniemenmaa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_areas_ceded_in_1940.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
